   122	**(1d) The `r = 1` ODE.** Prop 4.6, p.170 (`SOURCE-READ`): if `r = 1` then
   123	`D(n, −M_1, g_σ(π), T^ψ_{1,σ}(π)) = nonzero constant`, with
   124	`D(a,b,p,q) = a p q' − b q p'` (Def 4.1, p.164). *Jacobian entry #3, and it
   125	is the root of all the others.* Prop 4.1, p.164:
   126	```
   127	   J_{t,π}(h(σ), g(σ)) = [λ_h h_σ g'_σ − λ_g g_σ h'_σ] t^{λ_g+λ_h−1} + ⋯
   128	                       = J_{f,g}(h,g)·J_{x,y}(f,g)·J_{t,π}(x,σ)
   129	                       = h_f(σ) · 1 · (−t^{δ−2}) .
   130	```
   131	The `1` **is** `J_{x,y}(f,g)`. Matching `t`-orders of the two displayed
   132	lines is precisely Prop 4.6(3): `(n−M_r)λ = −1 + δ`; Moh spells this
   133	equivalence out on p.170. With `r = 1`, `λ_g = nλ`, `λ_h = (−M_1)λ`,
   134	`ord h_f(σ) = (−μ_1+M_1)λ = 0`, the bracket is `−λ·D(n,−M_1,g_σ,T_{1,σ})`
   135	and the leading coefficient of `h_f(σ)` is a nonzero constant, giving
   136	`D(…) = nonzero constant`.
   137	
   138	**(1e) Coprimality.** From `D(n,−M_1,g_σ,T_{1,σ}) = c ≠ 0` directly: a
   139	common root `ξ` of `g_σ, T_{1,σ}` gives `D(ξ) = 0`; a common root of
   140	`g'_σ, T'_{1,σ}` likewise. So
   141	`(g_σ,T_{1,σ}) = 1` **and** `(g'_σ,T'_{1,σ}) = 1` (p.187). Prop A.5,
   142	p.207 (`SOURCE-READ`) gives the strictly stronger
   143	`g_σ(π)·T_{1,σ}(π)` **has only simple roots** — Moh states A.5 here but
   144	uses only the two coprimalities.
